# ADR-018: Audit Logging Strategy

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| ADR | 018 |
| Title | Audit Logging Strategy |
| Status | Accepted |
| Date | 2026-07-27 |
| Decision Makers | R-zension Engineering Team |

---

# Context

R-zension manages sensitive organizational data, rankings, scores, goals, user accounts, and AI-generated insights.

To ensure security, accountability, compliance, and operational transparency, the platform requires a comprehensive audit logging system.

Audit logs are not intended for debugging. They serve as an immutable historical record of significant business and security events.

---

# Decision

R-zension will implement a centralized audit logging system that records all security-sensitive and business-critical actions across the platform.

Audit logs are append-only and cannot be modified by application users.

---

# Objectives

The audit logging system should

- Track important user actions
- Record administrative changes
- Support forensic investigations
- Improve security visibility
- Enable compliance reporting
- Maintain complete accountability

---

# Architecture

```text
User Action

↓

Business Service

↓

Business Operation

↓

Audit Event

↓

Audit Logger

↓

PostgreSQL Audit Table

↓

Analytics / Monitoring
```

Audit logging should occur after successful business operations whenever possible.

---

# Audit Scope

The system should log

- Authentication events
- Authorization failures
- Organization changes
- User management
- Goal management
- Score changes
- Ranking updates
- Role assignments
- Permission changes
- Administrative actions
- AI requests
- File operations
- Configuration changes

---

# Authentication Events

Examples

- Login
- Logout
- Failed login
- Password change
- Password reset
- Token refresh
- MFA events (Future)

---

# User Management Events

Examples

- User created
- User updated
- User deleted
- User suspended
- User restored
- Role changed

---

# Organization Events

Examples

- Organization created
- Organization updated
- Member invited
- Member removed
- Organization archived

---

# Ranking Events

Examples

- Rank changed
- Manual score adjustment
- Leaderboard recalculated
- Seasonal reset

---

# Oracle AI Events

Examples

- AI request submitted
- Recommendation generated
- Prediction requested
- AI service failure

Only metadata should be logged.

Prompts and sensitive AI context should not be stored unless explicitly required.

---

# File Events

Examples

- Upload
- Download
- Delete
- Restore
- Export

---

# Audit Record Structure

Each audit record should include

- Audit ID
- Timestamp
- User ID
- Organization ID
- Action
- Resource Type
- Resource ID
- Request ID
- IP Address
- User Agent
- Service
- Status
- Metadata

---

# Example Record

```json
{
  "auditId": "AUD-81F4A2",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-27T18:20:45Z",
  "organizationId": "ORG-201",
  "userId": "USR-983",
  "action": "GOAL_COMPLETED",
  "resourceType": "Goal",
  "resourceId": "GL-284",
  "status": "SUCCESS",
  "requestId": "REQ-91FA22"
}
```

---

# Immutability

Audit logs are append-only.

Application users

- Cannot edit
- Cannot delete
- Cannot overwrite

Only retention policies may archive or purge logs after the configured retention period.

---

# Retention Policy

Recommended retention

| Audit Type | Retention |
|------------|-----------|
| Authentication | 1 Year |
| Security | 3 Years |
| Administrative | 5 Years |
| Business Operations | 3 Years |

Retention periods may vary according to legal and organizational requirements.

---

# Search & Filtering

Audit logs should support filtering by

- User
- Organization
- Date range
- Resource
- Action
- Status
- Request ID

This enables efficient investigations.

---

# Correlation IDs

Every audit record should include a request or correlation ID.

Example

```text
REQ-91FA22
```

This allows tracing activity across multiple services.

---

# Security

Audit logs should never contain

- Passwords
- JWT tokens
- API keys
- Encryption keys
- Secrets
- Sensitive personal information unless required

Sensitive metadata should be masked where appropriate.

---

# Access Control

Audit logs are visible only to

- Platform Administrators
- Organization Administrators (tenant-scoped)
- Security Officers (Future)

Standard users cannot access audit records.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- Audit events per minute
- Failed audit writes
- Storage growth
- Security event frequency
- Administrative activity
- Suspicious behavior

---

# Performance Goals

| Metric | Target |
|---------|---------|
| Audit Write | < 50 ms |
| Audit Query | < 200 ms |
| Write Success Rate | > 99.99% |

Audit logging should not noticeably increase request latency.

---

# Storage Strategy

Audit logs are stored separately from operational business data.

Future enhancements may move audit records to

- Data warehouse
- Log analytics platform
- SIEM
- Cold storage

Operational tables should remain optimized for transactional workloads.

---

# Failure Handling

If audit logging fails

- Retry logging
- Record the failure
- Notify monitoring systems

Critical business operations should continue unless compliance requirements mandate otherwise.

---

# Compliance

The audit logging system is designed to support

- Internal governance
- Security investigations
- Operational accountability
- Compliance reporting

Specific regulatory requirements may be added in future deployments.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Application Logs Only

Pros

- Simple implementation

Cons

- Not structured
- Difficult searching
- Not suitable for compliance
- Easily rotated

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Database Triggers

Pros

- Automatic recording

Cons

- Difficult maintenance
- Limited business context
- Hard to extend

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Centralized Audit Service

Pros

- Consistent
- Structured
- Searchable
- Scalable
- Independent
- Compliance-friendly

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Complete accountability
- Better security investigations
- Easier compliance
- Improved operational visibility
- Strong forensic support

Negative

- Additional storage requirements
- Increased monitoring needs
- Long-term retention management

---

# Future Evolution

Future enhancements include

- Immutable storage
- Digital signatures
- SIEM integration
- Real-time anomaly detection
- Compliance dashboards
- AI-assisted threat detection
- Cross-service trace visualization
- Long-term archival policies

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts a centralized, append-only audit logging strategy that records business-critical and security-sensitive events across all services. Audit records provide accountability, traceability, operational visibility, and a strong foundation for security investigations and future compliance requirements.

